        "content": "# Feature: YAML Validator Command\n\n## Overview\nAdd a simple YAML validator command to the project that validates YAML content for syntax correctness without adding any new external dependencies.\n\n## Requirements\n\n### Functional Requirements\n- **FR-1**: Create a `validateYaml` function that accepts a YAML string and returns a validation result\n- **FR-2**: The validator must detect common YAML syntax errors (bad indentation, invalid characters, unclosed quotes, duplicate keys)\n- **FR-3**: Return structured results with `valid` boolean, and `errors` array with line numbers and messages when invalid\n- **FR-4**: Create a CLI command entry point that reads YAML from a file path argument or stdin\n- **FR-5**: Exit with code 0 on valid YAML, non-zero on invalid YAML\n- **FR-6**: Print human-readable error messages to stderr on validation failure\n\n### Non-Functional Requirements\n- **NFR-1**: No new external dependencies - use only Bun built-ins and custom parsing logic\n- **NFR-2**: Follow existing project patterns (camelCase functions, PascalCase types, co-located tests)\n- **NFR-3**: Include comprehensive unit tests using Bun's test runner\n\n### Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] `validateYaml()` correctly identifies valid YAML strings\n- [ ] `validateYaml()` correctly identifies invalid YAML with descriptive errors\n- [ ] CLI command `bun run validate-yaml <file>` works for file input\n- [ ] No new entries in package.json dependencies\n- [ ] All tests pass via `bun test`\n- [ ] Follows existing code style and patterns\n"
