        "content": "# Feature: YAML Validator Command\n\n## Feature ID\nyaml-validator-command\n\n## Summary\nAdd a simple YAML validator command to the CLI that validates YAML file syntax without adding new dependencies.\n\n## Requirements\n\n### FR-1: YAML Validation Function\n- Implement a `validateYaml(input: string)` function that parses a YAML string and returns a validation result\n- Must use only Bun built-in capabilities or hand-written parsing (no new npm dependencies)\n- Return a structured result with `valid: boolean`, `error?: string`, and `lineNumber?: number`\n\n### FR-2: CLI Command Entry Point\n- Add a `validate-yaml` command accessible via `bun run src/index.ts validate-yaml <file-path>`\n- Read the file from the given path and validate its YAML content\n- Print a success message if valid, or an error message with details if invalid\n- Exit with code 0 on valid YAML, code 1 on invalid YAML or file errors\n\n### FR-3: Error Reporting\n- Report the nature of the YAML syntax error (e.g., bad indentation, invalid characters, unclosed quotes)\n- Include line number in error output when possible\n\n### NFR-1: No New Dependencies\n- Must NOT add any new entries to package.json dependencies or devDependencies\n- Use only Bun built-ins, Node.js built-ins available in Bun, or hand-written code\n\n### NFR-2: Testing\n- Unit tests for the validation function covering valid YAML, invalid YAML, and edge cases\n- Tests must follow existing patterns: co-located `*.test.ts` files using `bun:test`\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] `validateYaml()` correctly identifies valid YAML strings\n- [ ] `validateYaml()` correctly identifies invalid YAML with error details\n- [ ] CLI command reads a file and reports validation result\n- [ ] CLI exits with appropriate exit codes (0 for valid, 1 for invalid/error)\n- [ ] No new dependencies added to package.json\n- [ ] All tests pass with `bun test`\n"
